        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.20,
      "why": "unrolled_k emits one result every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, so it needs Fmax >= 50*(ceil(N/k)+3) MHz to hit 50 MSPS. For small k (large cycle count) this forces a high Fmax and high power, but for large k (few cycles) the required Fmax drops and it can be competitive on power with a smaller datapath. I include it with a modest share to check whether any (k, n_iter) combination lands on the front; if it cannot meet throughput at acceptable power it will simply fall off the Pareto front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The hard constraint is throughput_msps >= 50, which is the binding filter. iterative (N+3 cycles/result) and unrolled_k (ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result) only meet it by running at a high Fmax, which raises power_index; pipelined and pipelined_m give 1 result/cycle and are the natural throughput-safe families. pipelined_m is the most promising because its m parameter trades FF/area (and thus power) against latency while preserving identical bit-accuracy, so it spans the power-vs-accuracy trade-off the hypervolume rewards. I therefore weight pipelined_m (0.45) and pipelined (0.35) heavily, and keep unrolled_k (0.20) as a probe for any low-cycle/high-k point that might undercut them. Accuracy is the trade-off objective, so I center data_width (14..18) and n_iter (12..16) around the 2^-12 error target with enough range to map both the minimum-power feasible point and the higher-accuracy end of the front. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept low (0..2) since they add area/power without helping the throughput constraint, and rounding is left to the search. This allocation spends the budget where the feasible trade-offs actually are, leaving the reserved front-mapping pass to refine the full ranges of whichever families land on the Pareto front."
}

